<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Emirate of Asir</h1><p class="st-meta">1823 CE – 1872 CE (approx.) · capital Abha</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A highland emirate of south-western Arabia that resisted Egyptian and Ottoman armies for half a century from its base at Abha.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asir">Asir</a>, the mountainous region between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hejaz">Hejaz</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Yemen</a>, had joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-diriyah">Emirate of Diriyah</a> and fought hard against the Egyptian armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diriyah">Diriyah</a> fell in 1818, local leaders took charge, and from 1823 a series of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>, from 1834 of the Al Aidh family, ruled the region from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abha">Abha</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> led a confederation of highland tribes, combining Wahhabi-influenced religious leadership with tribal warfare. They fought off Egyptian expeditions in the 1820s and 1830s and raided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tihama">Tihama</a> coast and the Yemeni ports. Under A'id ibn Mar'i and his son Muhammad the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> remained largely independent of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> despite repeated campaigns.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1871-1872 an Ottoman army took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abha">Abha</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-aid">Muhammad ibn A'id</a> was killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asir">Asir</a> was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yemen-vilayet">Yemen Vilayet</a> as a sanjak, and later passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/idrisid-emirate-of-asir">Idrisid Emirate of Asir</a> and then to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/emirate-of-asir.jpg" alt="Emirate of Asir" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: Abo Yemen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Emirate_of_Asir.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1823 CE – 1872 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Abha</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ali ibn Mujaththal, A'id ibn Mar'i, Muhammad ibn A'id</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (Wahhabi)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Saudi Arabia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Asir under the Al Aidh, Asiri Emirate</dd></dl></aside></div>

